"The timing errors are simulated, and labeled so. Each press of the hand stopwatch, at the start and at the stop, is early or late by a random amount drawn from a normal distribution with standard deviation {#} s; the display reads to {#} s. The photogate has no reaction time and reads to {#} s. Read to the bottom of the bob (diameter {#} cm), a length reading is {#} cm longer than the true length from the pivot to the bob’s center.",
"The random draws repeat when the page is opened again: the same steps give the same numbers. <strong>New random draws</strong> (under Lab) moves to another set.",
"Use the tools of a statistics course on a physics measurement: check that {#} hand timings of a pendulum follow a normal distribution, give a confidence interval for g, test whether the data agree with g = {#} m/s², and use two sample tests to decide whether two ways of measuring give the same answer.",
"<strong>A pendulum gives g.</strong> For a small swing the period is \\(T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}\\), so",
"\\[g = \\frac{4\\pi^2 L}{T^2}.\\]",
"With L = {#} m and g = {#} m/s² the period would be \\(T_0 = 2\\pi\\sqrt{1.000/9.81} = 2.0061\\) s.",
"<strong>Why hand timings scatter normally.</strong> A hand timing is the true time plus the error of the stop press minus the error of the start press. Each press error is random, and the sum or difference of two normal errors is itself normal. A histogram of many timings should then look like a bell, and the <strong>normal distribution</strong> with the same mean and standard deviation should describe it: about {#} % of the timings within one standard deviation \\(s\\) of the mean and about {#} % within \\(2s\\). A <strong>normal quantile plot</strong> is a sharper check: put the timings in order and plot the i-th smallest against the z score a normal distribution would give it, \\(z = \\Phi^{-1}\\big((i - \\tfrac12)/n\\big)\\). Normal data fall close to a straight line, of slope \\(s\\); a curve or bent ends mean the data are skewed or have long tails.",
"<strong>A t interval for the mean.</strong> The standard deviation of the population is not known, so we estimate it with the sample’s \\(s\\) and use Student’s t distribution with \\(n - 1\\) degrees of freedom. A {#} % confidence interval for the mean period \\(\\mu_T\\) is",
"\\[\\bar T \\pm t^* \\frac{s}{\\sqrt n},\\]",
"with \\(t^* = 1.984\\) for {#} degrees of freedom (\\(t^* = 2.262\\) for {#}). Since \\(g = 4\\pi^2L/T^2\\) falls as T rises, the interval for g runs from \\(4\\pi^2 L/T_{\\text{high}}^2\\) up to \\(4\\pi^2 L/T_{\\text{low}}^2\\). We work with the periods, not with g, because it is the timings that are normal.",
"<strong>A t test of one mean.</strong> To test \\(H_0: \\mu_T = T_0\\) (the data agree with g = {#} m/s²) against \\(H_a: \\mu_T \\ne T_0\\), compute",
"\\[t = \\frac{\\bar T - T_0}{s/\\sqrt n}\\]",
"and the p value, the chance of a \\(\\lvert t \\rvert\\) this large or larger if \\(H_0\\) is true (two tails of the t distribution with \\(n - 1\\) degrees of freedom; use a calculator’s tcdf or a table). If \\(p < \\alpha = 0.05\\), reject \\(H_0\\).",
"<strong>Two means.</strong> To compare two independent samples (two methods of measuring), test \\(H_0: \\mu_1 = \\mu_2\\) with",
"\\[t = \\frac{\\bar x_1 - \\bar x_2}{\\sqrt{s_1^2/n_1 + s_2^2/n_2}},\\]",
"the book’s unpooled (Welch) test. Its degrees of freedom are",
"\\[df = \\frac{\\left(s_1^2/n_1 + s_2^2/n_2\\right)^2}{\\dfrac{(s_1^2/n_1)^2}{n_1 - 1} + \\dfrac{(s_2^2/n_2)^2}{n_2 - 1}},\\]",
"which a calculator’s {#}-SampTTest works out for you. If one sample has no spread at all (\\(s_2 = 0\\)), this is the same as a one sample test of \\(\\bar x_1\\) against \\(\\bar x_2\\), with \\(df = n_1 - 1\\).",
"The mean \\(\\bar g\\) of the g worked out from each timing comes out a little above \\(4\\pi^2 L/\\bar T^2\\), because \\(4\\pi^2L/T^2\\) bends: a short timing raises g more than a long one lowers it. Two methods that time the same way share this, so it cancels when you compare them.",
"A test can only find a difference that shows up in the data. A length read from the wrong place changes every value of g in the same way: no amount of repeating at one setup reveals it, but comparing two methods can.",
